WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.543
This is the executive briefing
for Book 13 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.543 --> 00:00:08.324
Modernization Workbook:
Governance, Drift and Evidence.

4
00:00:08.324 --> 00:00:13.615
It's for the people who fund federal
systems, sign for them or answer for them:

5
00:00:13.615 --> 00:00:18.137
the CIO, the CISO, authorizing
officials and the program office.

6
00:00:18.137 --> 00:00:19.837
The book asks one question.

7
00:00:19.837 --> 00:00:22.600
Why does compliance decay
the day after the audit?

8
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.422
Its answer is that an
assessment measures one day,

9
00:00:25.422 --> 00:00:28.175
and the estate starts
changing the next morning.

10
00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:32.578
I'll take four parts: why it
decays, what keeps the record true,

11
00:00:32.578 --> 00:00:35.363
who governs it, and how it runs at scale.

12
00:00:35.363 --> 00:00:39.762
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

13
00:00:39.762 --> 00:00:42.160
Let's start with the decay itself.

14
00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.898
Picture the week a package is signed.

15
00:00:44.898 --> 00:00:46.723
On that day, it's all true.

16
00:00:46.723 --> 00:00:50.749
The next morning someone changes
a firewall rule to fix an outage,

17
00:00:50.749 --> 00:00:55.597
a contractor leaves but the account stays
on, and a provider ships a new default.

18
00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:57.373
None of it reaches the package.

19
00:00:57.373 --> 00:01:01.654
The book traces this to four
structural failures, not to people:

20
00:01:01.654 --> 00:01:06.266
copies of facts that disagree,
telemetry combined by hand, evidence

21
00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:11.372
rebuilt at every audit, and policy
trapped inside the tool that enforces it.

22
00:01:11.372 --> 00:01:13.372
Each fix sits beneath the tools.

23
00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:18.034
So the decision for the CIO: fix that
layer before buying another tool.

24
00:01:18.034 --> 00:01:21.460
Next, what federal
policy already asks for.

25
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:27.510
FISMA makes annual testing a floor, not
a ceiling, and expects automated tools.

26
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:32.004
NIST treats monitoring as ongoing
awareness, and its framework ends with

27
00:01:32.004 --> 00:01:36.498
the authorizing official reviewing
posture all the time, not once a cycle.

28
00:01:36.498 --> 00:01:42.798
Circular A-130 tells agencies to move
eligible systems to ongoing authorization.

29
00:01:42.798 --> 00:01:44.660
So the destination is settled.

30
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.364
What lags is practice, and NIST says why:

31
00:01:47.364 --> 00:01:50.998
without substantial
automation, it isn't practical.

32
00:01:50.998 --> 00:01:55.185
The usual answer to findings is more
checkpoints and more reviewers.

33
00:01:55.185 --> 00:01:58.460
That makes reporting heavier
and leaves the drift alone.

34
00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.523
The decision for the CISO:
answer the next findings

35
00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:04.372
by making evidence
regenerable from the record.

36
00:02:04.372 --> 00:02:07.320
Next, the mechanism behind the decay.

37
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.659
Drift is any gap between
what the record says

38
00:02:10.659 --> 00:02:13.458
should be true and what
the estate actually does.

39
00:02:13.458 --> 00:02:18.774
It gets in five ways: unauthorized change,
approved change that's never written

40
00:02:18.774 --> 00:02:24.171
back, provider change, people who move or
leave, and tools that update themselves.

41
00:02:24.171 --> 00:02:28.058
Most of it is an administrator fixing
an outage at two in the morning.

42
00:02:28.058 --> 00:02:32.821
It's continuous and silent: every system
keeps reporting what it was told.

43
00:02:32.821 --> 00:02:34.644
And it's the opening attacks use:

44
00:02:34.644 --> 00:02:38.783
an account that should have been
disabled, a permission nobody removed.

45
00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:42.982
The baseline and change-control
controls already require finding it.

46
00:02:42.982 --> 00:02:48.257
The decision for the CISO: make drift a
standing finding in the monitoring strategy.

47
00:02:48.257 --> 00:02:51.710
Next, the record drift
is measured against.

48
00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.131
Drift can only be measured
against something,

49
00:02:55.131 --> 00:02:57.872
so the program keeps one
authoritative record.

50
00:02:57.872 --> 00:03:00.263
It holds desired state, the rules,

51
00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:04.511
and an authority map that says
which system owns each fact.

52
00:03:04.511 --> 00:03:08.274
HR owns who a worker is, the
asset registry owns devices,

53
00:03:08.274 --> 00:03:12.372
and infrastructure as code
owns declared infrastructure.

54
00:03:12.372 --> 00:03:16.484
Everything else is a pointer back,
and runtime systems only read it.

55
00:03:16.484 --> 00:03:20.560
Corrections flow upstream to the
owning source, never to the copy.

56
00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.837
A write to a demoted copy is critical,

57
00:03:22.837 --> 00:03:25.948
because it's a second source
of truth growing back.

58
00:03:25.948 --> 00:03:29.493
The decision: settle the record
before the next migration,

59
00:03:29.493 --> 00:03:33.260
so the migration changes who
supplies a fact, not what it means.

60
00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.440
Next, how the record is allowed to change.

61
00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:40.165
A record is only as good
as the path into it.

62
00:03:40.165 --> 00:03:44.853
So every change, from a move
to a reset, travels one path.

63
00:03:44.853 --> 00:03:49.171
It starts from the source of truth,
gets approved by the right authority,

64
00:03:49.171 --> 00:03:52.245
runs under a role that's
switched on just for the job,

65
00:03:52.245 --> 00:03:54.953
closes with proof, and
emits its own evidence.

66
00:03:54.953 --> 00:03:59.477
Skip a step and it isn't a change with
a missing form; it's a drift finding.

67
00:03:59.477 --> 00:04:01.577
There's no second lane for urgent work.

68
00:04:01.577 --> 00:04:05.478
Pre-approved lanes are standing
grants with a scope and an expiry.

69
00:04:05.478 --> 00:04:09.740
Emergencies get a path, and they're
written back within one business day.

70
00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:14.052
The decision for the steward: make the
protections bind administrators too.

71
00:04:14.052 --> 00:04:17.660
Next, one question every
change must answer.

72
00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:23.348
Retiring Active Directory takes most of
a year, and the estate keeps changing.

73
00:04:23.348 --> 00:04:26.925
Every new domain-joined
server, legacy application,

74
00:04:26.925 --> 00:04:31.585
service account or Group Policy
setting is one more thing to unwind.

75
00:04:31.585 --> 00:04:35.334
So from gate G0, every add and
change answers one question:

76
00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:38.923
does this create a new
dependency on Active Directory?

77
00:04:38.923 --> 00:04:41.935
Microsoft's own guidance
puts that step first.

78
00:04:41.935 --> 00:04:46.547
A yes isn't automatically a no; some
workloads have no modern path yet.

79
00:04:46.547 --> 00:04:49.974
But a yes becomes an exception,
with an owner, a reason,

80
00:04:49.974 --> 00:04:53.798
an end date and the Entra
alternative that was considered.

81
00:04:53.798 --> 00:04:56.921
The decision belongs to
the authorizing official,

82
00:04:56.921 --> 00:04:59.673
because keeping AD open
is a risk acceptance.

83
00:04:59.673 --> 00:05:02.630
Next, what closing a change requires.

84
00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:05.430
The strictest step is closure.

85
00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.983
A task closed by someone
clicking complete,

86
00:05:07.983 --> 00:05:11.742
with no machine-readable check
attached, says the work happened.

87
00:05:11.742 --> 00:05:13.967
It proves nothing an assessor can use.

88
00:05:13.967 --> 00:05:18.180
So every closure carries its check,
the time, and what did the checking.

89
00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:22.888
Human-only closure stays possible,
because operations need an escape hatch,

90
00:05:22.888 --> 00:05:25.205
but it's flagged and counted on its own.

91
00:05:25.205 --> 00:05:28.405
And reports are derived
from the closure records by

92
00:05:28.405 --> 00:05:31.530
something anyone can re-run,
never compiled by hand.

93
00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.027
A pipeline that can't
tell a checked closure

94
00:05:34.027 --> 00:05:36.455
from an asserted one is attesting fiction.

95
00:05:36.455 --> 00:05:41.282
The decision for the CISO: report
human-only closures as their own count,

96
00:05:41.282 --> 00:05:44.742
never blended into control
tests or service levels.

97
00:05:44.742 --> 00:05:47.890
Next, evidence an assessor can re-run.

98
00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.840
An attestation tells an
assessor who's accountable.

99
00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.327
Evidence lets them find
out for themselves.

100
00:05:54.327 --> 00:05:56.542
The program's rule is determinism:

101
00:05:56.542 --> 00:06:01.290
the same record and the same inputs
produce the same evidence, every time.

102
00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.678
So verification becomes a
re-run, not an interview.

103
00:06:04.678 --> 00:06:06.889
Federal guidance goes most of the way.

104
00:06:06.889 --> 00:06:12.483
NIST treats an automated comparison of
intended and actual state as assessment

105
00:06:12.483 --> 00:06:15.822
results, and FedRAMP's
2026 rules tell assessors

106
00:06:15.822 --> 00:06:19.078
not to simply review
documents or screenshots.

107
00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:22.596
Some controls stay attestations,
like a training record

108
00:06:22.596 --> 00:06:25.890
or a contract clause, and
the package should say which.

109
00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:30.769
The decision for the authorizing
official: give assessors their own access,

110
00:06:30.769 --> 00:06:33.790
so the assessor, not the
operator, does the re-run.

111
00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.880
Next, the drift engine.

112
00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:40.385
The drift engine compares the
estate with a versioned record,

113
00:06:40.385 --> 00:06:44.730
so an approved change isn't drift and
any finding can be re-derived later.

114
00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:49.818
Severity is computed from the kind of drift
and whether the thing is in active use.

115
00:06:49.818 --> 00:06:52.990
A critical finding halts
and alerts a named person,

116
00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.180
and it's never fixed automatically.

117
00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.362
The engine never edits the record,

118
00:06:57.362 --> 00:07:01.180
never judges whether a rule is
right, and never accepts risk.

119
00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.993
And be precise with assessors:
some checks are automated today,

120
00:07:04.993 --> 00:07:07.193
and others are still found by people.

121
00:07:07.193 --> 00:07:11.795
The riskiest change is to what counts as
drift, so the decision: any change to

122
00:07:11.795 --> 00:07:16.843
the classifier or its thresholds gets a
reviewer from outside the program office.

123
00:07:16.843 --> 00:07:18.830
Next, who governs.

124
00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:24.942
A platform can detect every drift and still
decay, because it can't decide anything.

125
00:07:24.942 --> 00:07:26.480
Each finding is a question.

126
00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:27.692
Change the estate?

127
00:07:27.692 --> 00:07:28.829
Change the record?

128
00:07:28.829 --> 00:07:30.605
Or carry the difference for a while?

129
00:07:30.605 --> 00:07:32.893
The last two need a person with authority.

130
00:07:32.893 --> 00:07:35.005
The book keeps two jobs apart.

131
00:07:35.005 --> 00:07:38.496
The steward keeps the record,
and information owners,

132
00:07:38.496 --> 00:07:42.305
like HR or an application owner,
decide what its facts mean.

133
00:07:42.305 --> 00:07:44.935
The CISO decides control requirements,

134
00:07:44.935 --> 00:07:48.030
and only the authorizing
official accepts risk.

135
00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.805
The governance board
reviews; it doesn't vote,

136
00:07:50.805 --> 00:07:55.080
because a board that decides by
consensus leaves no one accountable.

137
00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.351
The decision for the sponsor and
CIO: charter the board in writing,

138
00:07:59.351 --> 00:08:02.593
with one named decider for
every kind of decision.

139
00:08:02.593 --> 00:08:04.470
Next, exceptions.

140
00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:08.233
A finding that can't be
fixed in time is carried,

141
00:08:08.233 --> 00:08:10.532
by a named decision, with an end date.

142
00:08:10.532 --> 00:08:14.503
A bounded exception has an end
date and a compensating control,

143
00:08:14.503 --> 00:08:16.107
and the CISO approves it.

144
00:08:16.107 --> 00:08:21.270
Anything open-ended, or with no
compensating control, is a risk acceptance.

145
00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.835
The CISO recommends, the
authorizing official decides,

146
00:08:24.835 --> 00:08:26.807
and it goes on the plan of action.

147
00:08:26.807 --> 00:08:30.663
The rule never changes; the engine
keeps reporting the difference,

148
00:08:30.663 --> 00:08:34.869
marked as excepted, so an excepted
setting never looks like a passing one.

149
00:08:34.869 --> 00:08:38.645
The decision for the board: review
the register every quarter.

150
00:08:38.645 --> 00:08:40.728
Nothing renews automatically,

151
00:08:40.728 --> 00:08:45.295
and anything renewed twice goes back
up as a sign the rule must change.

152
00:08:45.295 --> 00:08:48.240
Next, the authorizing official's part.

153
00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:53.277
The authorizing official sits outside
the program and above the record.

154
00:08:53.277 --> 00:08:56.828
The role is small in time
and large in consequence.

155
00:08:56.828 --> 00:08:58.848
The program asks four things of it:

156
00:08:58.848 --> 00:09:01.733
sign for every gap it
carries rather than closes;

157
00:09:01.733 --> 00:09:06.711
keep reviewing whether the risk is still
acceptable; decide whether a significant

158
00:09:06.711 --> 00:09:11.328
change needs reauthorization; and set
how independent the assessors must be.

159
00:09:11.328 --> 00:09:16.310
Their representative can do the daily work,
but risk acceptance can't be delegated,

160
00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.314
and a contractor can prepare
one but never sign it.

161
00:09:19.314 --> 00:09:23.602
The decision for the authorizing
official: receive a monthly posture

162
00:09:23.602 --> 00:09:27.815
report built from the record, and
sign every open-ended gap in writing.

163
00:09:27.815 --> 00:09:30.660
Next, the record as a federal record.

164
00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:35.160
The record is a federal record,
whether or not anyone calls it one.

165
00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.814
Its history shows who changed controlled
settings, who approved them and when,

166
00:09:39.814 --> 00:09:43.322
which is exactly what change
control asks an agency to keep.

167
00:09:43.322 --> 00:09:47.545
So the records officer decides how
long each kind of record lasts,

168
00:09:47.545 --> 00:09:50.985
the steward carries it out,
and the CISO is consulted.

169
00:09:50.985 --> 00:09:56.047
Change records, for example, are kept
five years after a system is retired.

170
00:09:56.047 --> 00:10:00.973
The one thing that undoes the repository's
protections is rewriting its history.

171
00:10:00.973 --> 00:10:02.898
A purge destroys records.

172
00:10:02.898 --> 00:10:07.548
If a secret is committed by mistake,
it's revoked and rotated first.

173
00:10:07.548 --> 00:10:10.420
The decision: history
is rewritten only with

174
00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:13.448
the records officer's
and the CISO's agreement.

175
00:10:13.448 --> 00:10:16.160
Next, the tempo of operations.

176
00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:20.288
A platform that detects
perfectly and closes slowly

177
00:10:20.288 --> 00:10:23.435
produces an accurate list
of things nobody fixed.

178
00:10:23.435 --> 00:10:26.022
Wherever a machine can reach the system,

179
00:10:26.022 --> 00:10:28.922
the ticket authorizes a
machine, not a person.

180
00:10:28.922 --> 00:10:30.234
A human approves.

181
00:10:30.234 --> 00:10:33.424
The platform runs a versioned
plan after a dry run,

182
00:10:33.424 --> 00:10:36.147
then re-reads the result
to prove it worked.

183
00:10:36.147 --> 00:10:41.048
Some changes stay gated forever, like
Conditional Access or tenant-wide roles.

184
00:10:41.048 --> 00:10:44.554
Where no machine can reach,
the fallback is a declared,

185
00:10:44.554 --> 00:10:48.936
recorded session with an end date,
never a quiet trip to the console.

186
00:10:48.936 --> 00:10:51.379
Letting a whole class run without a person

187
00:10:51.379 --> 00:10:54.172
accepts the risk of being
wrong at machine speed.

188
00:10:54.172 --> 00:10:57.534
So the authorizing official
accepts each one in writing.

189
00:10:57.534 --> 00:11:00.290
Next, reuse across agencies.

190
00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.902
The federal estate assesses the
same controls again and again.

191
00:11:04.902 --> 00:11:08.331
OMB's memorandum on FedRAMP
makes reuse the default:

192
00:11:08.331 --> 00:11:12.397
an agency presumes a maintained
FedRAMP package is adequate,

193
00:11:12.397 --> 00:11:16.303
and asks for more only with a
documented, demonstrable need.

194
00:11:16.303 --> 00:11:19.752
But reuse always ends in
the agency's own signature.

195
00:11:19.752 --> 00:11:24.327
Each agency authorizes its own system,
never a standalone authorization

196
00:11:24.327 --> 00:11:28.527
for the cloud service, and reviews
each provider's quarterly report.

197
00:11:28.527 --> 00:11:33.603
FedRAMP's 2026 indicators
bind providers, not agencies.

198
00:11:33.603 --> 00:11:35.990
What the program adds
is the evidence side.

199
00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:39.024
If the shared package is
produced continuously,

200
00:11:39.024 --> 00:11:41.602
reuse stops meaning last year's snapshot.

201
00:11:41.602 --> 00:11:43.671
The decision: reuse by default,

202
00:11:43.671 --> 00:11:47.727
and document any demonstrable
need before asking for more.

203
00:11:47.727 --> 00:11:50.320
Next, ongoing authorization.

204
00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:54.470
Reciprocity reuses a
package across agencies.

205
00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:59.920
Ongoing authorization reuses it across
time, and that's what makes the first safe.

206
00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:03.303
NIST sets two conditions:
a complete initial review,

207
00:12:03.303 --> 00:12:08.182
and monitoring that checks the controls
as often as the strategy requires.

208
00:12:08.182 --> 00:12:12.495
The authorizing official then issues
a new decision with no end date.

209
00:12:12.495 --> 00:12:16.795
They review posture on a schedule, and
right away when something triggers it.

210
00:12:16.795 --> 00:12:19.320
Reauthorization becomes the exception.

211
00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:24.495
Automation is necessary but not enough;
people still assess what tools can't see.

212
00:12:24.495 --> 00:12:30.258
Circular A-130 tells agencies to make
this move when a system is eligible.

213
00:12:30.258 --> 00:12:35.215
The decision for the authorizing official:
approve each eligible system's move,

214
00:12:35.215 --> 00:12:38.595
with how often it's reviewed
and what triggers a review.

215
00:12:38.595 --> 00:12:40.950
Next, governance at scale.

216
00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.845
At the scale of hundreds of systems,

217
00:12:43.845 --> 00:12:47.025
the program's discipline
is honesty about coverage.

218
00:12:47.025 --> 00:12:50.702
Every control is covered,
partial, a gap, or out of scope,

219
00:12:50.702 --> 00:12:54.713
and partial is the answer most
often rounded up to covered.

220
00:12:54.713 --> 00:12:58.363
Every partial or gap goes
into a register that names,

221
00:12:58.363 --> 00:13:01.249
on day one, the evidence
that will close it.

222
00:13:01.249 --> 00:13:04.811
A gap closes by configuration,
by a substitute control,

223
00:13:04.811 --> 00:13:08.925
or by the authorizing official
accepting it with a review date.

224
00:13:08.925 --> 00:13:13.813
Coverage is reported as proven and
asserted counts, never one percentage.

225
00:13:13.813 --> 00:13:16.600
The decision for the
senior official for risk:

226
00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:19.388
approve the agency-wide
monitoring strategy.

227
00:13:19.388 --> 00:13:21.840
Now, the decisions together.

228
00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:26.577
So, the decisions together, and
none of them is a procurement.

229
00:13:26.577 --> 00:13:30.465
The CIO fixes the layer beneath
the tools before buying another.

230
00:13:30.465 --> 00:13:35.702
The CISO makes drift a standing finding and
counts human-only closures on their own.

231
00:13:35.702 --> 00:13:38.250
The steward settles the
record before the next

232
00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:41.452
migration and makes the
protections bind administrators.

233
00:13:41.452 --> 00:13:46.603
The sponsor and CIO charter the board,
with one decider for every decision.

234
00:13:46.603 --> 00:13:51.630
The authorizing official approves each
new Active Directory dependency only as a

235
00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:55.419
dated exception, accepts each
class that runs without a person,

236
00:13:55.419 --> 00:13:58.989
and approves ongoing authorization
when a system is ready.

237
00:13:58.989 --> 00:14:01.190
The records officer guards the history.

238
00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:03.065
A package describes one day.

239
00:14:03.065 --> 00:14:07.677
Governance that doesn't decay keeps
proving itself, every day after.

240
00:14:07.677 --> 00:14:08.500
Thank you.
